skills.theory:
Real programs rarely store only one flat list or one flat dictionary. They combine structures to represent records, tables, groups, and relationships.

A list of dictionaries is a common table-like model:
students = [
    {"name": "Ada", "score": 91},
    {"name": "Lin", "score": 84}
]
The list stores rows. Each dictionary gives one row named fields.

A dictionary of lists can represent columns:
table = {
    "name": ["Ada", "Lin"],
    "score": [91, 84]
}
This can be useful for column operations, but every column must remain aligned. Choose the representation that makes the required operations clearest.

Nested access proceeds one level at a time:
students[1]["score"]
The list index selects a record, then the dictionary key selects a field.

Nested loops traverse nested sequences:
for group in groups:
    for item in group:
        process(item)
Keep the loop roles clear with specific names such as group, row, field, and item.

Missing data needs a policy. Direct lookup record["email"] raises KeyError when the field is absent. record.get("email") returns None, or record.get("email", "unknown") returns an explicit default. A default is appropriate only when it has a clear meaning.

Aliasing matters more in nested structures. dictionary.copy() and list.copy() create shallow outer copies. Nested lists and dictionaries are still shared. Use copy.deepcopy only when independent recursive duplication is actually required, and understand that copying is not a substitute for a clear ownership model.

Useful record-processing pattern:
1. validate the outer collection;
2. validate each record and required field;
3. normalize field values;
4. aggregate or transform;
5. return a new result instead of mutating input unexpectedly.

Common mistake:
Do not assume every nested record has the same valid shape merely because the first one does. Validate all rows that the algorithm uses.

Another common mistake:
Do not accidentally reuse one mutable template dictionary for every row. Create a fresh record for each item.

