skills.theory:
An algorithm is not described only by whether it eventually produces the right answer. Input size, required work, memory, clarity, and preconditions also matter.

Linear search checks items in order until it finds the target or reaches the end. For n items, the worst case inspects n items. It works on unsorted sequences and can stop early.

Binary search repeatedly compares the target with the middle of a sorted range. Each comparison discards about half of the remaining positions. Its work grows logarithmically, but only when the data is sorted using the same ordering and supports indexed access.

Sorting APIs:
- sorted(iterable) returns a new list;
- list.sort() changes one list in place and returns None;
- reverse=True selects descending order;
- key=function computes the value used for ordering.

Example:
sorted(words, key=len)
orders words by length. Python's sort is stable: items with equal keys keep their previous relative order.

Big-O notation describes broad growth as input size becomes large:
- O(1): roughly constant work;
- O(log n): repeated halving;
- O(n): one pass through n items;
- O(n log n): efficient comparison sorting;
- O(n^2): work for many pairs or two full nested passes;
- O(2^n): branching that roughly doubles with each added element.
Big-O ignores fixed constants and lower-order terms. It does not replace measurement or clear design.

Choose data structures around frequent operations. A set or dictionary usually provides direct membership or key lookup. A list preserves order and duplicates but may require a scan for membership. Sorting once can support many later ordered operations, but sorting for a single simple lookup may cost more than one scan.

Correctness comes first. Optimize only after identifying a meaningful bottleneck and preserving tests. A readable O(n) pass is often preferable to a complicated alternative when n is small.

Common mistake:
Do not apply binary search to unsorted input. Its decisions become invalid even if some examples happen to work.

Another common mistake:
Do not write result = values.sort(). result becomes None because sort mutates values rather than returning the list.

